            "impact": "If merchant-authored templates are not meant to execute arbitrary Jinja, a merchant can cause server-side evaluation of template expressions in the Flask/Jinja context when an order detail page is viewed. Depending on the runtime environment and what objects are reachable from that context, this can expose in-scope order or restaurant data and may permit broader template-engine abuse, including code execution paths commonly associated with SSTI.",
            "prerequisites": "- **Deployment conditions**\n  - An authenticated merchant must be able to save arbitrary receipt template text for their restaurant.\n  - The order detail route must later render that stored text.\n- **Default vs optional code**\n  - The code is part of the application's normal authenticated merchant routes in `app/routes/orders.py`; the provided context does not describe it as an optional extension.\n- **Trust boundary**\n  - The boundary is between **merchant-controlled persisted content** and **server-side Jinja evaluation**.\n  - The attacker model in the supplied trust-boundary notes is an authenticated restaurant user with a valid session cookie.\n- **Project context**\n  - The finding context explicitly calls out receipt templates as a recurring security concern in `receipts.py` and `orders.py`.\n  - The notes also state the rendered result is later autoescaped in HTML templates, so the main concern here is server-side template execution rather than browser-side XSS.\n- **Related CVE context**\n  - The pattern is similar in kind to prior Jinja template-execution issues such as **CVE-2016-10745** in that attacker-controlled template content reaches a Jinja execution context.\n  - It is not the same mechanism described in that CVE, because the provided code does not show `SandboxedEnvironment` usage or the specific Python `format` escape path; the similarity is the attacker-controlled server-side template evaluation precondition.",
            "recommendation": {
