                "text": "### Description\nIn app/__init__.py within create_app, change the admin blueprint registration at line 23 so the admin routes are only registered when an explicit ENABLE_ADMIN_ROUTES configuration flag is enabled.\n\n### Suggested fix\n    from app.routes.auth_routes import bp as auth_bp\r\n    from app.routes.menus import bp as menus_bp\r\n    from app.routes.orders import bp as orders_bp\r\n    from app.routes.receipts import bp as receipts_bp\r\n    from app.routes.admin import bp as admin_bp\r\n\r\n    app.register_blueprint(auth_bp)\r\n    app.register_blueprint(menus_bp)\r\n    app.register_blueprint(orders_bp)\r\n    app.register_blueprint(receipts_bp)\r\n    if app.config.get(\"ENABLE_ADMIN_ROUTES\", False):\r\n        app.register_blueprint(admin_bp)\r\n\r\n    @app.route(\"/\")\r\n    def index():\r\n\n### Rationale\nConditionally registering the blueprint removes the admin endpoints from the default public HTTP surface, so unauthenticated callers cannot reach them unless a deployment deliberately enables that route set and can place compensating controls around it.\n\n### Verification\nBefore the change, start the app with default configuration and send an unauthenticated GET request to /admin/restaurants; the route is present and returns the admin listing. After the change, with ENABLE_ADMIN_ROUTES unset or false, the same request should no longer reach the admin handler and should return a missing-route response such as 404. If a deployment enables ENABLE_ADMIN_ROUTES, verify that separate authentication and authorization controls are in place for those routes."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
