            "debateSummary": "- **True positive votes:** 1\n- **False positive votes:** 0\n- **Review limitation:** the debate transcript shows a single-model review, so there was no multi-model disagreement to compare.\n\n**Arguments recorded for the finding**\n- `current_user()` reads `session[\"user_id\"]` at `app/auth.py:32` and loads a user row at line 33.\n- `login_required` at line 40 only checks whether that lookup returns a user object.\n- The finding context states `Config.SECRET_KEY` can fall back to the known literal `\"portal-dev-key\"`, and `create_app` loads that configuration, so Flask’s client-signed session cookie can be forged under that configuration.\n- Under those conditions, protected routes using this decorator can be reached without completing the login flow.\n\n**Arguments recorded against or limiting the finding**\n- The decorator itself is a standard session check and does not introduce the predictable secret.\n- If deployment sets a strong `MERCHANT_PORTAL_SECRET`, the reported bypass condition does not apply.\n- The scenario also depends on Flask using its default signed-cookie session mechanism and on the attacker choosing a valid `user_id`.\n\n**Unresolved disagreements**\n- No direct disagreement was recorded; the main limitation discussed was that exploitability depends on deployment configuration outside this function.",
