            },
            "prerequisites": "- **Deployment conditions**\n  - The application must run with the documented default `SECRET_KEY` fallback (`\"portal-dev-key\"`) or another predictable signing key.\n  - Flask session cookies must remain client-side signed tokens.\n  - The attacker must be able to mint a valid session containing a chosen `user_id`, and that id must map to an existing user row.\n\n- **Code inclusion**\n  - This is core application code in `app/auth.py`, not an optional or loadable extension.\n\n- **Trust boundary**\n  - The relevant boundary is between **public HTTP clients** and **authenticated merchant session routes**.\n  - The decorator is intended to enforce that boundary for wrapped views, but its decision is based entirely on session-backed identity.\n\n- **Project context**\n  - The inline security annotation on this function states a deployment-unique `SECRET_KEY` is a precondition before treating `current_user()` as authenticated, and marks that precondition as violated by the default config path.\n  - The broader project security notes also describe hard-coded default secrets and seeded credentials as a separate concern affecting authentication state.\n\n- **Related CVE note**\n  - The listed `CVE-2021-23336` concerns URL query parsing ambiguities in `urllib.parse`; it is not a close variant of this session-authentication finding.",
            "recommendation": {
              "remediationBackwardCompat": "### Description\nTreat this decorator as dependent on a trusted session layer. The concrete mitigation is in configuration rather than in `login_required` itself: remove the literal `SECRET_KEY` fallback, require a deployment-unique secret at startup, and invalidate or rotate existing sessions when changing the key. If that configuration guarantee is not present, routes relying only on this decorator inherit the weakened authentication boundary.\n\n### Rationale\nCVSS v3.1 base score 6.5 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:N, confidence: medium) — AV:N (the attacker sends a forged Flask session cookie to HTTP endpoints protected by @login_required); AC:L (when the app uses the fallback \"portal-dev-key\", minting a cookie with a chosen session['user_id'] requires no external condition beyond that vulnerable configuration); PR:N (the decorator accepts the forged session without any prior authenticated privileges); UI:N (no victim interaction is needed); S:U (auth.py and the protected merchant routes are within the same application and database authority); C:L (bypassing the decorator makes merchant-session routes that expose tenant menu, order, and receipt data reachable as the chosen user); I:L (the same bypass reaches protected editing/status-update routes that rely on this decorator as the gate for tenant changes); A:N (the evidence shows unauthorized access, not a direct service outage, from the decorator bypass itself).\n\n### Verification\nTrigger: A forged Flask session cookie containing attacker-chosen session[\"user_id\"]\nPath: `session.get(\"user_id\")` -> `db.get_user` -> `current_user`\nTo verify: address the condition above and re-scan; confirm this finding no longer reproduces.",
