              "remediationBackwardCompat": "### Description\nIn app/db.py, change get_menu_item at line 102 to require the current restaurant context and add `restaurant_id = ?` to the SELECT predicate so the lookup is tenant-scoped.\n\n### Suggested fix\n    return db.execute(query).fetchall()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef get_menu_item(item_id, restaurant_id=None):\r\n    db = get_db()\r\n    if restaurant_id is None:\r\n        return None\r\n    return db.execute(\r\n        \"SELECT * FROM menu_items WHERE id = ? AND restaurant_id = ?\",\r\n        (item_id, restaurant_id),\r\n    ).fetchone()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef update_menu_item(item_id, name, price_cents, available):\r\n\n### Rationale\nAdding the restaurant identifier to the WHERE clause binds the row fetch to the authenticated tenant, so a guessed or enumerated item ID by itself is no longer sufficient to retrieve another restaurant's record.\n\n### Verification\nBefore the change, call the menu edit flow as restaurant A with an `item_id` that belongs to restaurant B and observe that the record is returned. After the change and after updating callers to pass the current restaurant's ID, the same request should return no row or the route's not-found/forbidden handling, while restaurant A can still load its own item IDs.",
