                "text": "### Description\nIn app/routes/admin.py, add authentication and an object-level authorization check to toggle_restaurant at line 15 so the route only proceeds for a logged-in user whose restaurant_id matches the target restaurant_id.\n\n### Suggested fix\nfrom flask import Blueprint, abort, redirect, render_template, request, url_for\r\n\r\nfrom app import db\r\nfrom app.auth import current_user, login_required\r\n\r\nbp = Blueprint(\"admin\", __name__, url_prefix=\"/admin\")\r\n\r\n    return render_template(\"admin_restaurants.html\", restaurants=rows)\r\n\r\n\r\n@bp.route(\"/restaurants/<int:restaurant_id>/toggle\", methods=[\"POST\"])\r\n@login_required\r\ndef toggle_restaurant(restaurant_id):\r\n    user = current_user()\r\n    if user is None or user[\"restaurant_id\"] != restaurant_id:\r\n        abort(403)\r\n\r\n    restaurant = db.get_restaurant(restaurant_id)\r\n    if restaurant is not None:\r\n        db.set_restaurant_active(restaurant_id, not restaurant[\"active\"])\r\n    return redirect(url_for(\"admin.restaurants\"))\r\n\n### Rationale\nThe added login_required gate blocks unauthenticated requests, and comparing the session-backed principal to the URL target prevents the handler from using an arbitrary caller-supplied restaurant_id to select and update another restaurant's record.\n\n### Verification\nBefore the change, POST /admin/restaurants/2/toggle can toggle restaurant 2 when sent without a session or from a session associated with a different restaurant. After the change, unauthenticated requests should be rejected by the login guard and a logged-in user whose current_user()[\"restaurant_id\"] does not equal 2 should receive 403, while a matching authorized user can still toggle their own restaurant."
