            "debateSummary": "- Models voting **true positive**: 1\n- Models voting **false positive**: 0\n\nKey points raised:\n- The handler at `app/routes/menus.py:54` receives `item_id` from the URL and does not call `current_user()` or compare `item[\"restaurant_id\"]` to the session restaurant before updating.\n- The source annotations state that `db.get_menu_item()` in `app/db.py:102-106` selects by `id` alone and `db.update_menu_item()` in `app/db.py:109-116` updates with `WHERE id = ?` only.\n- The only visible guard is `@login_required`, which authenticates the caller but does not show object-level ownership enforcement.\n\nUnresolved points:\n- The debate transcript noted the possibility of an outer ownership control or a different database-layer restriction, but no such control appears in the provided evidence.\n\nReview limitation:\n- The review was effectively unchallenged; the transcript shows a single model analysis rather than a multi-model split.",
