                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "def edit_item(item_id):\n    user = current_user()\n    item = db.get_menu_item(item_id)\n    if item is None or item[\"restaurant_id\"] != user[\"restaurant_id\"]:\n        flash(\"That item no longer exists.\")\n        return redirect(url_for(\"menus.menu\"))"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "### Description\nIn app/routes/menus.py in edit_item at line 54, load the current user and reject requests where the fetched menu item is missing or its restaurant_id does not match the authenticated user's restaurant_id before rendering or updating the record.\n\n### Suggested fix\n@bp.route(\"/menu/item/<int:item_id>/edit\", methods=[\"GET\", \"POST\"])\r\n@login_required\r\ndef edit_item(item_id):\r\n    user = current_user()\r\n    item = db.get_menu_item(item_id)\r\n    if item is None or item[\"restaurant_id\"] != user[\"restaurant_id\"]:\r\n        flash(\"That item no longer exists.\")\r\n        return redirect(url_for(\"menus.menu\"))\r\n\r\n    if request.method == \"POST\":\r\n        name = request.form.get(\"name\", item[\"name\"]).strip()\r\n\n### Rationale\nChecking the loaded row's restaurant_id against the session's restaurant_id enforces object-level authorization at the route, so an authenticated user can only edit menu items belonging to their own tenant.\n\n### Verification\nBefore the change, log in as restaurant A and POST to /menu/item/<item_id>/edit using an item_id that belongs to restaurant B; the other restaurant's item is updated. After the change, the same request should redirect with the existing \"That item no longer exists.\" message and the foreign row should remain unchanged, while edits to restaurant A's own items should still succeed."
              }
            }
          ]
