            "impact": "If the fallback signing key is active, a forged session cookie could let a client act as a user from another restaurant and make `set_status()` update that tenant's order state to any value allowed by `ALLOWED_STATUSES`. The direct effect is integrity impact on order workflow data across tenant boundaries.",
            "noFixReason": {
              "category": "not_isolable",
              "reason": "The flagged line in app/routes/orders.py is a by-design status update after an ownership check. The reported weakness depends on a separate configuration issue: Flask session integrity can fail if the application uses the fallback SECRET_KEY 'portal-dev-key'. An isolated edit to set_status() would not prevent forged sessions signed with the same configured key from being trusted by current_user(). The concrete fix is in the configuration/bootstrap code that sets SECRET_KEY, not in the shown route body."
            },
            "prerequisites": "- Required conditions:\n  - The deployment uses the fallback Flask `SECRET_KEY` (`\"portal-dev-key\"`) or another known/predictable signing key.\n  - The application uses Flask signed-cookie sessions for authentication state.\n  - The attacker can obtain or guess a valid user identifier for a target restaurant and a target `order_id`.\n- Code/deployment context:\n  - `set_status()` is part of the normal authenticated merchant route set, not an optional extension.\n  - The state change at line 84 is by design; the finding concerns the trustworthiness of the session-derived identity used before that update.\n- Trust boundary:\n  - The boundary crossed is from a client-controlled session cookie to server-side tenant authorization logic.\n  - The attacker model in the provided trust-boundary context is a remote web client interacting with authenticated merchant routes.\n- Project context:\n  - The supplied project security concerns also mention default secrets and seeded credentials as a broader authentication/session-hardening issue.\n  - No related CVE was listed in the provided finding context.",
            "recommendation": {
              "remediationBackwardCompat": "### Description\nRemove the fallback `SECRET_KEY` behavior and require a deployment-unique secret before the application starts. Because `set_status()` bases tenant authorization on `current_user()`, any route using that pattern depends on the integrity of the session-signing configuration; after secret hardening, the existing restaurant ownership check at lines 75-77 remains the control that scopes the update.\n\n### Rationale\nCVSS v3.1 base score 5.3 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N, confidence: medium) — AV:N (the harmful operation is POST /orders/<order_id>/status on a network-reachable route, and a forged Flask session cookie lets a remote caller reach db.update_order_status()); AC:L (once the known fallback secret is in use, exploitation is a straightforward forged-session request with no race or target-specific condition); PR:N (the attacker needs no pre-existing account because current_user() is derived from the forgeable session cookie); UI:N (the attacker can send the status-changing request directly); S:U (the unauthorized update stays within the same application/database security authority); C:N (the demonstrated sink changes status but does not directly disclose data to the attacker); I:L (db.update_order_status(order_id, new_status) directly makes an unauthorized cross-tenant workflow-state change, limited to an order record); A:N (the evidence shows no direct service interruption or resource exhaustion).\n\n### Verification\nTrigger: A forged Flask session cookie that controls current_user(), made possible when the session-signing SECRET_KEY falls back to the literal \"portal-dev-key\", letting the attacker choose a restaurant_id.\nPath: `set_status` -> `current_user` -> `user[\"restaurant_id\"]` -> `db.update_order_status`\nTo verify: address the condition above and re-scan; confirm this finding no longer reproduces.",
              "remediationSteps": {
                "description": "Remove the fallback `SECRET_KEY` behavior and require a deployment-unique secret before the application starts. Because `set_status()` bases tenant authorization on `current_user()`, any route using that pattern depends on the integrity of the session-signing configuration; after secret hardening, the existing restaurant ownership check at lines 75-77 remains the control that scopes the update.",
                "rational": "CVSS v3.1 base score 5.3 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N, confidence: medium) — AV:N (the harmful operation is POST /orders/<order_id>/status on a network-reachable route, and a forged Flask session cookie lets a remote caller reach db.update_order_status()); AC:L (once the known fallback secret is in use, exploitation is a straightforward forged-session request with no race or target-specific condition); PR:N (the attacker needs no pre-existing account because current_user() is derived from the forgeable session cookie); UI:N (the attacker can send the status-changing request directly); S:U (the unauthorized update stays within the same application/database security authority); C:N (the demonstrated sink changes status but does not directly disclose data to the attacker); I:L (db.update_order_status(order_id, new_status) directly makes an unauthorized cross-tenant workflow-state change, limited to an order record); A:N (the evidence shows no direct service interruption or resource exhaustion).",
                "rationale": "CVSS v3.1 base score 5.3 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N, confidence: medium) — AV:N (the harmful operation is POST /orders/<order_id>/status on a network-reachable route, and a forged Flask session cookie lets a remote caller reach db.update_order_status()); AC:L (once the known fallback secret is in use, exploitation is a straightforward forged-session request with no race or target-specific condition); PR:N (the attacker needs no pre-existing account because current_user() is derived from the forgeable session cookie); UI:N (the attacker can send the status-changing request directly); S:U (the unauthorized update stays within the same application/database security authority); C:N (the demonstrated sink changes status but does not directly disclose data to the attacker); I:L (db.update_order_status(order_id, new_status) directly makes an unauthorized cross-tenant workflow-state change, limited to an order record); A:N (the evidence shows no direct service interruption or resource exhaustion).",
                "verification": "Trigger: A forged Flask session cookie that controls current_user(), made possible when the session-signing SECRET_KEY falls back to the literal \"portal-dev-key\", letting the attacker choose a restaurant_id.\nPath: `set_status` -> `current_user` -> `user[\"restaurant_id\"]` -> `db.update_order_status`\nTo verify: address the condition above and re-scan; confirm this finding no longer reproduces."
