            "function": "Dockerfile:<global>",
            "impact": "If the prerequisites are met, code executing through the web process runs as root inside the container. That allows modification of application files under `/app`, the local SQLite database path referenced in the review context, and any writable mounted data. It also increases the consequences of any separate container escape or lateral-movement condition, though this finding alone does not imply host-level root access.",
            "prerequisites": "- **Deployment conditions required:**\n  - The image must be run as built, without an external runtime override such as `docker run --user`, Kubernetes `securityContext.runAsUser`, or an equivalent entrypoint privilege drop.\n  - An attacker must first obtain code execution inside the web process through another application issue.\n- **Default vs optional code path:**\n  - This is the main `Dockerfile` for the application image, not an optional extension.\n- **Trust boundary crossed:**\n  - The boundary is from a low-trust remote web user to code execution inside the containerized server process.\n  - The supplied context identifies authenticated merchant routes as low trust and cites merchant-controlled receipt template rendering as one server-side execution path.\n- **Threat model / documentation context:**\n  - The trust-boundary data describes authenticated merchant inputs as low trust.\n  - No repository evidence was provided showing that running the container as root is an intentional requirement.\n- **Related CVEs:**\n  - No related CVEs were listed in the provided finding context.",
