              "description": {
                "text": "### Description\nIn migrations/init_db.py within seed() at lines 72-80, stop seeding source-coded salt literals and generate a fresh random salt for each inserted user record before calling hash_password().\n\n### Suggested fix\n            (name, email),\r\n        )\r\n\r\n    import secrets\r\n\r\n    accounts = [\r\n        (1, \"owner@b021bistro.io\", \"bistro123\"),\r\n        (2, \"owner@noodlebot.io\", \"noodle123\"),\r\n    ]\r\n    for restaurant_id, email, password in accounts:\r\n        salt = secrets.token_hex(16)\r\n        conn.execute(\r\n            \"INSERT INTO users (restaurant_id, email, password_hash, \"\r\n            \"password_salt) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)\",\r\n            (restaurant_id, email, hash_password(password, salt), salt),\r\n        )\r\n\r\n    items = [\r\n        (1, \"Truffle Fries\", 650),\r\n\n### Rationale\nA per-record random salt prevents reusable precomputation across accounts and deployments, because the stored verifier is derived from an unpredictable value that differs for each password row.\n\n### Verification\nBefore the change, initialize two databases with migrations/init_db.py and compare the password_salt values for the seeded users; they are the same literals in both databases. After the change, rerun the script twice and verify that each seeded user receives a different high-entropy password_salt value on each initialization while login still succeeds with the seeded password for that run."
              }
            }
