            "dataFlowTrace": "`<top-level>` -> `create_app` -> `app.config.from_object`\n\n- **[ENTRY]** `<top-level>`  `run.py:5`\n  Application startup calls create_app() without passing a safer configuration object, so the default Config path is used.\n  - **[FLOW]** `create_app`  `app/__init__.py:7`\n    The config_object parameter defaults to Config, which keeps the built-in configuration active when no override is supplied.\n  - **[FLAG]** `app.config.from_object`  `app/__init__.py:9`\n    Flask imports settings from Config, including SECRET_KEY if the environment variable was left unset.",
            "dataFlowValidation": "strict",
            "debateConsensus": "TP",
            "debateSummary": "- **True positive votes:** 1\n- **False positive votes:** 0\n- The review noted that `run.py` invokes `create_app()` without a safer override, so the default `Config` path is reachable in normal startup.\n- The main counterargument raised was that the fallback may be intended for local development and that exploitation still requires knowledge of Flask session contents and valid user identifiers.\n- The same review concluded that there is no startup guard in the shown code preventing the default secret from being used outside a demo context.\n- This was an **unchallenged single-model review**, so there was no multi-model disagreement to resolve.",
            "end_line": 7,
            "fileSourceUrl": "https://github.com/caldova-io/merchant-portal/blob/47bbff4de1fa369ff4fcac75139b2203096b29d1/app/__init__.py#L7",
