            "function": "app/__init__.py:create_app",
            "impact": "If the fallback secret is active, an attacker who knows that value can generate or modify Flask-signed session cookies. In this application, the debate evidence states that authentication relies on session-backed identity, so forged session state could allow impersonation of other users or modification of session-associated data.",
            "prerequisites": "- `MERCHANT_PORTAL_SECRET` must be unset, empty, or otherwise leave the `\"portal-dev-key\"` fallback in effect.\n- The application must be running in a context where remote clients can send Flask session cookies back to the server.\n- `run.py:5` calls `create_app()` directly, so this path appears to be part of the default startup flow rather than an optional extension.\n- The trust boundary is between the deployment/operator setting application secrets and remote clients relying on signed session state; if the known default remains configured, remote clients who know that value can attempt to forge session material.\n- The provided project notes describe the bootstrap and demo setup as potentially local/demo-oriented, but the supplied evidence does not show a dev-only gate or startup refusal when the default secret remains configured.\n- No related CVE references were provided in the finding context.",
            "recommendation": {
