            "impact": "If the fallback secret remains in use, a remote client could create a valid session cookie containing a chosen `user_id` and reach routes protected only by `login_required()`. That would allow access as the selected application user and whatever data or actions are available to that account.",
            "prerequisites": "- The deployment must leave the Flask secret unset so the application uses the fallback value `\"portal-dev-key\"` described in the finding context.\n- The application must be using the normal Flask signed client-side session mechanism without an additional server-side session validation layer.\n- `app/auth.py` is part of the core authentication path, not an optional extension.\n- The trust boundary crossed is the unauthenticated web client to authenticated merchant-session boundary: public HTTP requests become trusted as an authenticated user if the attacker can mint a valid session cookie.\n- The code in `login_required()` is otherwise by-design for a session-backed Flask application; the security question depends on whether the session signing secret is deployment-unique.\n- Project context in the supplied notes indicates the bootstrap/demo setup includes insecure defaults, so exposure depends on deployment hygiene rather than a hidden feature flag.",
            "recommendation": {
              "codeAfter": "    session.clear()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef current_user():\r\n    from flask import current_app\r\n\r\n    if current_app.config.get(\"SECRET_KEY\") == \"portal-dev-key\":\r\n        session.clear()\r\n        g.user = None\r\n        return None\r\n\r\n    if \"user\" not in g:\r\n        user_id = session.get(\"user_id\")\r\n        g.user = db.get_user(user_id) if user_id else None\r\n    return g.user\r\n\r\n\r\ndef login_required(view):\r",
              "codeBefore": "    session.clear()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef current_user():\r\n    if \"user\" not in g:\r\n        user_id = session.get(\"user_id\")\r\n        g.user = db.get_user(user_id) if user_id else None\r\n    return g.user\r\n\r\n\r\ndef login_required(view):\r",
              "fixDiff": "--- a/app/auth.py\n+++ b/app/auth.py\n@@ -27,11 +27,18 @@\n     session.clear()\r\n \r\n \r\n-def current_user():\r\n-    if \"user\" not in g:\r\n-        user_id = session.get(\"user_id\")\r\n-        g.user = db.get_user(user_id) if user_id else None\r\n-    return g.user\r\n+def current_user():\r\n+    from flask import current_app\r\n+\r\n+    if current_app.config.get(\"SECRET_KEY\") == \"portal-dev-key\":\r\n+        session.clear()\r\n+        g.user = None\r\n+        return None\r\n+\r\n+    if \"user\" not in g:\r\n+        user_id = session.get(\"user_id\")\r\n+        g.user = db.get_user(user_id) if user_id else None\r\n+    return g.user\r\n \r\n \r\n def login_required(view):",
              "remediationBackwardCompat": "### Description\nIn app/auth.py, update current_user() at lines 30-34 to reject session-backed authentication when the application is still using the shipped default SECRET_KEY \"portal-dev-key\".\n\n### Suggested fix\n    session.clear()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef current_user():\r\n    from flask import current_app\r\n\r\n    if current_app.config.get(\"SECRET_KEY\") == \"portal-dev-key\":\r\n        session.clear()\r\n        g.user = None\r\n        return None\r\n\r\n    if \"user\" not in g:\r\n        user_id = session.get(\"user_id\")\r\n        g.user = db.get_user(user_id) if user_id else None\r\n    return g.user\r\n\r\n\r\ndef login_required(view):\r\n\n### Rationale\nThis change stops the code from deriving an authenticated identity from a Flask session cookie when the signing key is a known built-in constant. By refusing to trust session data under that configuration, forged cookies signed with the default key no longer produce a valid current_user().\n\n### Verification\nBefore the change, start the app without setting the deployment secret so it uses \"portal-dev-key\", forge a Flask session cookie containing a valid user_id, and request a route protected by login_required; the request is treated as authenticated. After the change, repeat the same test: current_user() returns None, the session is cleared, and the protected route redirects to auth.login instead of running as that user.",
              "remediationSteps": {
