            "severityRationale": "CVSS v3.1 base score 6.5 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:N, confidence: medium) — AV:N (a remote client can send the forged Flask session cookie over HTTP to routes protected by login_required, and the trust boundary places those routes on the web surface); AC:L (with the fallback secret in use, exploitation is just signing a session carrying user_id so current_user() returns a row and the decorator accepts the request); PR:N (no prior account is needed before sending the forged cookie); UI:N (no separate user must act because the attacker drives the request); S:U (the effect stays inside the same app/session/database security authority); C:L (validator evidence and shared route context show login_required protects sensitive authenticated routes exposing tenant data); I:L (the forged authenticated session can reach authenticated handlers that modify tenant data); A:N (the demonstrated effect is auth bypass into protected routes, not a direct outage).",
            "severitySource": "validator",
            "specificityGap": "[insecure-defaults] Default semantics exist: the shared packet states Config falls back to literal \"portal-dev-key\" at config.py:7 when no secret is configured. This is a security-sensitive session-signing key. The fallback is unsafe because a known built-in key lets attackers mint valid session cookies. No compensating gate appears here: line 40 only checks whether current_user() resolves from session data, so the insecure default remains effective out of the box.",
            "summary": "`login_required()` in `app/auth.py` allows access whenever `current_user()` resolves a user from `session[\"user_id\"]`. The flagged issue is not the decorator logic by itself, but that this trust decision depends on Flask session integrity, while the finding context states `config.py:7` falls back to the literal `SECRET_KEY` value `\"portal-dev-key\"` when no deployment secret is set. This becomes a security issue if the application is deployed with that fallback still active, because an external client could forge a signed session cookie and satisfy the authentication check.",
