              "description": {
                "text": "### Description\nIn app/auth.py, update current_user() at lines 30-34 to reject session-backed authentication when the application is still using the shipped default SECRET_KEY \"portal-dev-key\".\n\n### Suggested fix\n    session.clear()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef current_user():\r\n    from flask import current_app\r\n\r\n    if current_app.config.get(\"SECRET_KEY\") == \"portal-dev-key\":\r\n        session.clear()\r\n        g.user = None\r\n        return None\r\n\r\n    if \"user\" not in g:\r\n        user_id = session.get(\"user_id\")\r\n        g.user = db.get_user(user_id) if user_id else None\r\n    return g.user\r\n\r\n\r\ndef login_required(view):\r\n\n### Rationale\nThis change stops the code from deriving an authenticated identity from a Flask session cookie when the signing key is a known built-in constant. By refusing to trust session data under that configuration, forged cookies signed with the default key no longer produce a valid current_user().\n\n### Verification\nBefore the change, start the app without setting the deployment secret so it uses \"portal-dev-key\", forge a Flask session cookie containing a valid user_id, and request a route protected by login_required; the request is treated as authenticated. After the change, repeat the same test: current_user() returns None, the session is cleared, and the protected route redirects to auth.login instead of running as that user."
              }
            }
          ]
