            "prerequisites": "- **Required conditions**\n  - An operator runs the database bootstrap/seeding path.\n  - The seeded user accounts remain in the deployed database.\n  - Their default passwords are not rotated or forced to change.\n  - The public `/login` route is reachable.\n\n- **Default vs optional code path**\n  - `app/auth.py` is part of the main application.\n  - The insecure condition depends on use of the local bootstrap/seeding flow in `migrations/init_db.py`; the notes describe that script as appearing intended for local/demo setup.\n\n- **Trust boundary**\n  - The attacker is a remote unauthenticated web client submitting credentials to the public login route.\n  - The boundary crossed is from unauthenticated HTTP input to an authenticated restaurant-owner session if the known seeded password is still valid.\n\n- **Project context**\n  - The finding context explicitly calls out seeded credentials as a bootstrap/runtime authentication concern.\n  - The source annotation on `verify_password()` references historical repo context that this verifier will accept those seeded passwords if they remain deployed.",
            "recommendation": {
              "remediationBackwardCompat": "### Description\nIf these seeded accounts are meant only for demo/bootstrap use, avoid deploying them with fixed passwords in production-like environments. Practical options are to require operator-supplied initial passwords during setup, force a password change before first use, disable or remove demo accounts outside development, or refuse startup when known default credentials are detected.\n\n### Rationale\nCVSS v3.1 base score 6.5 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:N, confidence: medium) — AV:N (app/routes/auth_routes.py exposes public POST /login, and verify_password accepts the seeded password_hash/password_salt values for those accounts); AC:L (under the evidenced deployment condition of unchanged seeded defaults, exploitation is simply sending the known credentials, with no extra environmental dependency); PR:N (/login is reachable without prior authentication); UI:N (no victim interaction is needed to authenticate with the shipped credentials); S:U (the attacker gains a merchant session inside the same portal authority rather than crossing into a different security authority); C:L (that merchant session exposes the authenticated tenant's menu, order, and receipt-template data on merchant routes); I:L (the same session can perform authorized-for-owner but unauthorized-for-attacker changes such as menu or order updates for that tenant); A:N (the evidence shows account takeover and data access/modification, not direct availability loss).\n\n### Verification\nTrigger: A remote user submits the shipped seeded account credentials (for example the seeded restaurant owner email/password pair) after the operator initializes the database and leaves those defaults unchanged.\nPath: `verify_password` -> `expected` -> `candidate` -> `hmac.compare_digest`\nTo verify: address the condition above and re-scan; confirm this finding no longer reproduces.",
