            "severitySource": "validator",
            "specificityGap": "[insecure-defaults] Default semantics exist because migrations/init_db.py seed() inserts fixed user accounts and main() always invokes that seeding during baseline database initialization. This is a security-sensitive default-credentials setting, it weakens authentication by shipping known passwords, and there is no clear compensating gate such as dev-only enforcement or mandatory password rotation before use.",
            "summary": "`login()` reads `email` and `password` from `POST /login`, looks up the user by email, and authenticates the session when `auth.verify_password(user, password)` succeeds. The finding was flagged because the application’s initialization flow reportedly seeds fixed restaurant accounts with known passwords, and this handler is the public authentication path that accepts those credentials if they remain in the database. This becomes a security issue if the application is deployed with the seeded accounts intact and their passwords unchanged.",
