            "cwe_display": "CWE-798: Use of Hard-coded Credentials",
            "cwe_name": "Use of Hard-coded Credentials",
            "dataFlowTrace": "`login` -> `auth.verify_password` -> `auth.login_user`\n\n- **[ENTRY]** `login`  `app/routes/auth_routes.py:16`\n  An unauthenticated client reaches the login handler, which is one place where the application establishes authenticated session state.\n- **[FLOW]** `auth.verify_password`  `app/routes/auth_routes.py:21`\n  The submitted credentials are validated here, but no check on SECRET_KEY strength or uniqueness occurs in this path.\n- **[FLAG]** `auth.login_user`  `app/routes/auth_routes.py:22`\n  The handler writes user identity into Flask session state that will later be accepted if signed with the configured secret key.",
            "dataFlowValidation": "strict",
            "debateConsensus": "TP",
            "debateSummary": "- Available review result: 1 model argued true positive, 0 argued false positive; the provided transcript is single-sided rather than a multi-model disagreement.\n- Main technical argument: Flask signed-cookie session integrity depends on `SECRET_KEY`, authenticated routes trust session state such as the stored user identity, and the shipped fallback `portal-dev-key` is predictable if deployment leaves `MERCHANT_PORTAL_SECRET` unset.\n- Qualifier raised in the self-challenge: this depends on deployment configuration. If operators set a unique secret, forged cookies signed with the fallback value would not validate.\n- No opposing code-level argument was provided in the transcript.\n- The related-reference section lists a CWE-798 hard-coded-credentials KB pattern; no specific CVE was provided.",
            "end_line": 25,
            "fileSourceUrl": "https://github.com/caldova-io/merchant-portal/blob/47bbff4de1fa369ff4fcac75139b2203096b29d1/app/routes/auth_routes.py#L25",
            "firstDetected": "2026-08-27T12:55:16.879Z",
            "fixStatus": "declined",
            "function": "app/routes/auth_routes.py:login",
            "impact": "A remote client that knows the fallback secret can create a signed session cookie containing a chosen user identifier and have the application treat that session as authenticated. Resulting access would depend on the privileges of the forged `user_id`, and could expose or modify merchant-scoped functionality that relies on session identity.",
            "noFixReason": {
              "category": "not_isolable",
              "reason": "The finding stems from the application-wide SECRET_KEY fallback in config.py, while the shown file is only the login route that writes session state. Changing app/routes/auth_routes.py in isolation would not prevent other routes from trusting forged cookies, and no exact config.py snippet was provided here to record a byte-exact search/replace fix safely."
            },
            "prerequisites": "- Required conditions:\n  - `MERCHANT_PORTAL_SECRET` is unset.\n  - The app uses Flask’s signed-cookie session mechanism with that fallback secret.\n  - An attacker can send HTTP requests with a crafted session cookie.\n- Default vs optional:\n  - The finding summary states `app/__init__.py` loads `Config` unconditionally.\n  - The unsafe secret is described as the default when the operator does nothing, not as an optional extension.\n- Trust boundary:\n  - Public HTTP clients are unauthenticated at entry.\n  - Merchant routes later trust session-backed identity.\n  - The boundary crossed is between attacker-controlled cookie data and server-trusted authenticated session state when the signing key is predictable.\n- Project context:\n  - The trust-boundary notes describe public HTTP routes and authenticated merchant routes that rely on session state from `app/auth.py`.\n  - The security-context notes say the bootstrap appears aimed at local/demo setup, so exposure depends on deployment hygiene.",
            "recommendation": {
              "remediationBackwardCompat": "### Description\nRemove the predictable fallback for `SECRET_KEY` and require a deployment-specific unpredictable secret at startup, or gate any development-only default so it cannot be used outside a development environment. The `login()` session write is by design; the issue described here is the surrounding configuration that determines whether those session cookies can be forged.\n\n### Rationale\nCVSS v3.1 base score 6.5 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:N, confidence: medium) — AV:N (public HTTP routes are reachable remotely and Flask accepts the attacker-supplied session cookie over that network path); AC:L (if the fallback SECRET_KEY is active, forging a valid signed session with a chosen user_id requires no race or special target state); PR:N (the attacker needs no prior account because current_user() and login_required trust session['user_id'] from the cookie); UI:N (the attacker sends their own forged cookie and requests authenticated routes directly); S:U (the forged session is trusted within the same Flask app and SQLite-backed authority); C:L (the forged authenticated session grants unauthorized access to merchant dashboard/menu/order/receipt data for the impersonated user); I:L (the same impersonation lets the attacker invoke authenticated create/edit/status actions as that user); A:N (the evidence shows authentication bypass via session forgery, not direct service disruption).\n\n### Verification\nTrigger: A forged Flask session cookie signed with the fallback SECRET_KEY value 'portal-dev-key' when MERCHANT_PORTAL_SECRET is unset\nPath: `login` -> `auth.verify_password` -> `auth.login_user`\nTo verify: address the condition above and re-scan; confirm this finding no longer reproduces.",
              "remediationSteps": {
                "description": "Remove the predictable fallback for `SECRET_KEY` and require a deployment-specific unpredictable secret at startup, or gate any development-only default so it cannot be used outside a development environment. The `login()` session write is by design; the issue described here is the surrounding configuration that determines whether those session cookies can be forged.",
                "rational": "CVSS v3.1 base score 6.5 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:N, confidence: medium) — AV:N (public HTTP routes are reachable remotely and Flask accepts the attacker-supplied session cookie over that network path); AC:L (if the fallback SECRET_KEY is active, forging a valid signed session with a chosen user_id requires no race or special target state); PR:N (the attacker needs no prior account because current_user() and login_required trust session['user_id'] from the cookie); UI:N (the attacker sends their own forged cookie and requests authenticated routes directly); S:U (the forged session is trusted within the same Flask app and SQLite-backed authority); C:L (the forged authenticated session grants unauthorized access to merchant dashboard/menu/order/receipt data for the impersonated user); I:L (the same impersonation lets the attacker invoke authenticated create/edit/status actions as that user); A:N (the evidence shows authentication bypass via session forgery, not direct service disruption).",
