            "severitySource": "validator",
            "specificityGap": "[insecure-defaults] Config.SECRET_KEY defaults to a hardcoded fallback in config.py when the operator does nothing, and app/__init__.py loads that Config unconditionally. This is a security-sensitive default-credentials secret for session integrity, the default is unsafe because it is predictable, and there is no clear startup refusal or dev/test-only gate to prevent deployment with the fallback. (reported line 32 out of range for 'app/routes/auth_routes.py' (file has 31 lines); clamped to 25)",
            "summary": "`login()` in `app/routes/auth_routes.py` verifies submitted credentials and, on success, calls `auth.login_user(user)` at line 22 to establish Flask session-backed identity. The finding is tied to configuration rather than the password check itself: if `MERCHANT_PORTAL_SECRET` is unset, `Config.SECRET_KEY` falls back to the predictable value `portal-dev-key`, so a forged Flask session cookie can be accepted as authentic. The scanner location was clamped to line 25, but the session-establishment call in the shown source is line 22.",
