            "severitySource": "validator",
            "specificityGap": "[insecure-defaults] This is a default-credentials issue: seed() defines the initial authentication records directly in the shipped bootstrap data, and main() invokes seed() when initializing the database. The passwords are security-sensitive because they become the accepted login secrets after hashing, the unsafe direction is that the out-of-box accounts use predictable built-in passwords, and there is no clear compensating guard in this code such as dev/test-only gating, startup refusal until rotation, or forced password change.",
            "summary": "`seed()` provisions initial `users` records during database initialization and includes literal owner passwords in the shipped account list at lines 73-74. The flagged behavior is that these values are hashed and stored as active login verifiers at line 80, so a fresh deployment created from this script will accept known default credentials until they are changed. This matters if the bootstrap script is used outside a demo/local-only context and the seeded accounts remain enabled on a reachable application instance.",
