              "description": {
                "text": "### Description\nIn migrations/init_db.py within seed(), replace the hard-coded owner passwords at lines 73-74 with operator-supplied values from environment variables and abort initialization if either password is missing.\n\n### Suggested fix\n            (name, email),\r\n        )\r\n\r\n    env = __import__(\"os\").environ\r\n    accounts = [\r\n        (1, \"owner@b021bistro.io\", env.get(\"B021_OWNER_PASSWORD\"), \"b021\"),\r\n        (2, \"owner@noodlebot.io\", env.get(\"NOODLEBOT_OWNER_PASSWORD\"), \"ndle\"),\r\n    ]\r\n    if any(not password for _, _, password, _ in accounts):\r\n        raise RuntimeError(\r\n            \"Seed passwords must be supplied via B021_OWNER_PASSWORD and NOODLEBOT_OWNER_PASSWORD\"\r\n        )\r\n    for restaurant_id, email, password, salt in accounts:\r\n        conn.execute(\r\n            \"INSERT INTO users (restaurant_id, email, password_hash, \"\r\n            \"password_salt) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)\",\r\n\n### Rationale\nRemoving the shipped password literals prevents fresh deployments from creating accounts with publicly knowable secrets. Requiring an external password value at initialization time ensures the stored verifier is derived from deployment-specific credentials rather than a product default.\n\n### Verification\nBefore the change, run migrations/init_db.py on a fresh database and log in with owner@b021bistro.io / bistro123 or owner@noodlebot.io / noodle123; authentication succeeds. After the change, running the initializer without B021_OWNER_PASSWORD and NOODLEBOT_OWNER_PASSWORD should fail fast, and when those variables are set to deployment-specific values, the old default passwords should no longer authenticate while the supplied values should."
