            "impact": "If this code path is used in an exposed deployment with `DEBUG` unset, the application runs with Flask debug behavior rather than a hardened production baseline. Depending on the runtime and exposure, that can include detailed error output and debugger-related functionality that is intended for development use.",
            "prerequisites": "- The application must be started through `run.py` or equivalent code path that executes this block.\n- The `DEBUG` environment variable must be unset, or otherwise evaluate to a truthy value under the parser.\n- Remote exposure depends on deployment:\n  - By default, `HOST` is `127.0.0.1`, which limits reachability to the local machine.\n  - Exposure broadens if the operator changes `HOST`, publishes the service through container/network configuration, or places it behind a proxy.\n- The trust boundary here is operational configuration, not an HTTP request parameter. The relevant actor is the deployer/operator; a remote attacker only becomes relevant if the debug-enabled server is reachable.\n- In the provided context, this is top-level application startup code, not an optional extension or loadable module.\n- No project documentation was provided in this finding context stating that this debug-enabled default is restricted to development only.",
            "recommendation": {
              "codeAfter": "if __name__ == \"__main__\":\r\n    host = os.environ.get(\"HOST\", \"127.0.0.1\")\r\n    port = int(os.environ.get(\"PORT\", \"5000\"))\r\n    debug = os.environ.get(\"DEBUG\", \"false\").lower() in {\"1\", \"true\", \"yes\", \"on\"}\r\n    app.run(host=host, port=port, debug=debug)\r",
              "codeBefore": "if __name__ == \"__main__\":\r\n    host = os.environ.get(\"HOST\", \"127.0.0.1\")\r\n    port = int(os.environ.get(\"PORT\", \"5000\"))\r\n    debug = os.environ.get(\"DEBUG\", \"true\").lower() in {\"1\", \"true\", \"yes\", \"on\"}\r\n    app.run(host=host, port=port, debug=debug)\r",
              "fixDiff": "--- a/run.py\n+++ b/run.py\n@@ -7,5 +7,5 @@\n if __name__ == \"__main__\":\r\n     host = os.environ.get(\"HOST\", \"127.0.0.1\")\r\n     port = int(os.environ.get(\"PORT\", \"5000\"))\r\n-    debug = os.environ.get(\"DEBUG\", \"true\").lower() in {\"1\", \"true\", \"yes\", \"on\"}\r\n+    debug = os.environ.get(\"DEBUG\", \"false\").lower() in {\"1\", \"true\", \"yes\", \"on\"}\r\n     app.run(host=host, port=port, debug=debug)",
              "remediationBackwardCompat": "### Description\nIn run.py at line 10 in the top-level startup block, change the DEBUG environment fallback from \"true\" to \"false\" so Flask debug mode is disabled unless it is explicitly enabled.\n\n### Suggested fix\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\r\n    host = os.environ.get(\"HOST\", \"127.0.0.1\")\r\n    port = int(os.environ.get(\"PORT\", \"5000\"))\r\n    debug = os.environ.get(\"DEBUG\", \"false\").lower() in {\"1\", \"true\", \"yes\", \"on\"}\r\n    app.run(host=host, port=port, debug=debug)\r\n\n### Rationale\nUsing a safe default changes the startup baseline so an unset DEBUG variable no longer turns on Flask's debugger and other development behavior; the application only enters debug mode when an operator opts in through configuration.\n\n### Verification\nStart the application with DEBUG unset. Before the change, the computed debug value is True and Flask starts in debug mode. After the change, the computed debug value is False when DEBUG is unset, while setting DEBUG=true still enables debug mode explicitly.",
