            "severity": "Informational",
            "severityRationale": "CVSS v3.1 base score 0.0 (CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:N, confidence: medium) — AV:L (the demonstrated trigger is local startup of run.py with environment-derived settings, specifically DEBUG unset so it defaults true); AC:L (the code deterministically enables debug mode from the default value with no external condition); PR:L (the actor must control the local startup environment or invocation of the process); UI:N (no separate victim action is needed to enable the mode); S:U (the same Flask application is started in debug mode under the same security authority); C:N (the evidence here shows only debug-mode enablement, not a specific direct disclosure to an attacker); I:N (no demonstrated unauthorized modification or code execution is provided in this item); A:N (no direct availability impact is shown).",
            "severitySource": "validator",
            "specificityGap": "[insecure-defaults] This is a real fallback (`os.environ.get(\"DEBUG\", \"true\")`) on a security-sensitive flag, and the fallback is in the unsafe direction because it turns debug mode on by default. There is no code here that limits this default to a dev/test environment or refuses startup until the setting is hardened. Confidence is reduced because the same file defaults `HOST` to 127.0.0.1, which partially limits exposure unless deployment changes the host while leaving DEBUG unset.",
            "summary": "`run.py` reads startup settings from environment variables and passes them to `Flask.app.run()`. The scanner flagged line 10 because `DEBUG` defaults to `\"true\"` when unset, so the application starts with `debug=True` unless the operator overrides it. This becomes a security issue if this startup path is used outside development and the resulting server is exposed beyond the default loopback bind.",
            "toolCategory": "IaC",
            "triggeringInputShape": "The operator leaves the DEBUG environment variable unset, so the code falls back to \"true\".",
